Example 15 -- the Mona-Lisa problem (GPU-accelerated)
=====================================================

Goal
----
Evolve a set of alpha-blended circle-triangles whose superimposition resembles a target image. Each
candidate is a GImageIndividual: N triangles (10 parameters each: centre, radius, three angles, RGB,
alpha) plus a background colour. The fitness is the per-pixel, per-channel deviation of the rendered
candidate from the target image, passed through a rational saturation function -- the smaller, the
closer the resemblance.

What is new
-----------
This example is evaluated through the unified courtier GPU consumer
(Gem::Courtier::GPU::GGPUConsumerT), which lives in the Courtier library and is built as the optional
add-on `gemfony-courtier-gpu`. The GPU consumer is now a first-class, mnemonic-selectable consumer:
run with `--consumer gpu` to evaluate on the device. The example contributes only its device marshaller
via Go2::registerGPUConsumerBuilder(...); Go2 selects and builds the consumer like any other. Running
without `--consumer gpu` (e.g. the default `--consumer stc`) evaluates on the CPU through the
individual's fitnessCalculation(). A whole generation is flattened and scored in ONE bulk kernel launch.
The kernel (kernels/monalisa_eval.cu) is compiled at RUN TIME (NVRTC for CUDA), so:

  * the example builds with an ordinary C++ toolchain -- no CUDA language is enabled at build time;
  * the device-programming model AND the kernel are chosen in config/GGPUConsumer.json, so you can
    switch between CPU and CUDA without recompiling.

The kernel is pixel-parallel (256 threads cooperate on each candidate and atomic-accumulate its
fitness), so even a modest population fills the GPU. The SAME render+score math is also implemented on
the CPU (GImageIndividual::fitnessCalculation and the marshaller's host reference), so a CPU run
cross-checks the GPU result.

Because there is no network involved, this example doubles as a convenient stress-test harness for the
courtier framework, the broker and Hap.

Building
--------
The example is built automatically when libpng is available (it is skipped otherwise). The CUDA
backend is compiled into gemfony-courtier when a CUDA toolkit is present. The GPU consumer is
device-only; to run on the CPU, use a CPU consumer instead (e.g. --consumer stc), which evaluates via
the individual's own evaluate().

Running
-------
  ./GCUDAWorker --consumer gpu      # evaluate on the device (kernel chosen in GGPUConsumer.json)
  ./GCUDAWorker                     # default --consumer stc: evaluate on the CPU (individual's evaluate())

Useful options:
  --consumer gpu       Select the GPU consumer (omit to use the default CPU thread-pool consumer)
  --target <png>       The target image (default ./pictures/ml-small.png)
  --gpuConfig <json>   The GPU consumer config (default ./config/GGPUConsumer.json)
  --logImages <bool>   Write the best candidate image per iteration (default true)
  --emitBestOnly <bool> Only emit an image when the best result improved (default true)

To run on the CPU instead of the GPU, use a CPU consumer (e.g. --consumer stc): the GPU consumer is
device-only, so a CPU run evaluates through the individual's own evaluate() rather than the device
kernel. (The "backend" key in config/GGPUConsumer.json only accepts "cuda".)

Output
------
The best candidate of each iteration is rasterised and written to ./results/ as
<iteration>_<fitness>_bestIndividual.png, so the picture can be watched converging towards the target.

Configuration files (config/)
-----------------------------
  Go2.json                   top-level Go2 settings
  GEvolutionaryAlgorithm.json the evolutionary algorithm (population size, iterations, ...)
  GImageIndividual.json      the image individual (number of triangles, adaption, ...)
  GGPUConsumer.json          GPU consumer: backend (cpu|cuda), kernel path/entry, launch sizes
